        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Canyon lots, ocean bluffs and bay views</strong></p><p>San Diego sits on mesas cut by canyons, so a flat street often hides a backyard that drops away. Mission Hills, Bankers Hill, Kensington and Talmadge have rear decks on posts over the canyon. A frameless glass deck railing is the way to keep that view. On the coast, La Jolla, Bird Rock, Point Loma and Sunset Cliffs have bluff homes with ocean-view balconies and roof decks. Del Mar, Solana Beach, Encinitas and Carlsbad continue the same pattern up the North County coast.</p><p>Coronado and the downtown towers in Little Italy, the Marina District and East Village face the bay. Their condo balconies take a 42-inch or 48-inch glass balcony railing. Inland, Rancho Santa Fe, Poway, Scripps Ranch and Rancho Bernardo are pool country. There, the glass pool fence and 48-inch guards on raised patios are the usual projects.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Permits with Development Services, and the county inland</strong></p><p>Inside the city limits, from Point Loma to Rancho Bernardo, permits go through the City of San Diego Development Services Department. Coronado, Del Mar, Encinitas, Carlsbad and Poway each have their own building division. Rancho Santa Fe is unincorporated and goes through the County of San Diego. Homes in the coastal overlay zone may face an extra review step.</p><p>The code is the same everywhere. The California code sets a 42-inch minimum guard height, even on a single-family house (CRC R312.1). The state pool safety act sets pool enclosures at 60 inches high. Gaps must be under 4 inches, and the latch at least 60 inches above the ground. Our 39.6-inch, 46-inch and 57.7-inch panels give 42, 48 and 60 inches. Engineering documents for the spigot attachments are on our site. Start with the <a href=\"/pages/california-building-standards-code-for-glass-railings\">California code page</a> and our <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-california\">California hub</a>, then confirm with your building department.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Marine layer, salt air and delivery to a hillside street</strong></p><p>The marine layer rolls in most mornings from May through June, and salt air reaches a few miles inland. That is why every spigot, connector, hinge and latch is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise. Both finishes, Brushed Stainless and Matte Black, are on the same 2205 steel. The 12 mm tempered glass carries a lifetime warranty against manufacturing defects. Inland in Poway and Escondido the issue is heat, and tempered glass handles the sun without fading.</p><p>Your order ships from our warehouse after 2–3 business days of processing and arrives in 5–10 business days by freight carrier with liftgate service. Tell us if your street is steep or narrow, as in parts of Mission Hills or La Jolla. The carrier can then plan the stop. Report any shipping damage within 3 days, before installation, and we replace or refund the item at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
